WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.773
Increasing or decreasing a value by a percentage is one of the most common calculations in International GCSE Business. Let's start from the very beginning.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:19.286
Per cent means out of one hundred. So ten per cent means ten out of every hundred, which is the same as zero point one.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:28.298
Imagine a T-shirt that costs forty dollars. Ten per cent of forty dollars is forty times zero point one, which is four dollars.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:34.503
If the price rises by ten per cent, we add the four dollars on, giving forty-four dollars.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.924
If the price is cut by ten per cent, we take the four dollars away, giving thirty-six dollars.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:54.615
Before any working, decide the direction. Pay rises, interest added to a loan, tax added to a price, and growth all make the value bigger. Discounts, cuts and reductions make it smaller.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:07.147
Next, find the base: the value the percentage is taken from. If a customer buys several items, multiply first to get the full total. Then apply the percentage once, to that total.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:21.375
Then find the percentage amount: the base times the rate, divided by one hundred. Add it for an increase, or subtract it for a decrease. Round as the question asks, usually to two decimal places for money.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:41.833
There is also a quick route. For an increase, multiply by one plus the rate: a three point five per cent rise means times one point zero three five. For a decrease, multiply by one minus the rate: a twelve point five per cent discount means times zero point eight seven five.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:58.758
Here is a real exam question from Paper 1 in June 2024. A minimum wage of seven euros forty-five an hour rises by three point five per cent. Calculate the new hourly wage. It is worth two marks.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:11.617
Find three point five per cent of seven forty-five. Seven forty-five times zero point zero three five is zero point two six zero seven five, about twenty-six cents.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:18.472
Now add it on. Seven forty-five plus zero point two six gives seven euros seventy-one.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:29.247
Check with the quick route. Seven forty-five times one point zero three five is seven point seven one, to two decimal places. The same answer.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:38.051
The most common mistake is stopping too early. Twenty-six cents is only the increase. The question asks for the new wage.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:48.229
Another mistake is adding the percentage as if it were money: seven forty-five plus three point five. Always turn the percentage into an amount first.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:03:02.491
Now you try. A school orders twenty-five calculators at twelve dollars each. The supplier gives a fifteen per cent discount. Calculate the total cost after the discount. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.936
First the base. Twenty-five times twelve dollars is three hundred dollars. A discount is a decrease.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:26.009
Fifteen per cent of three hundred is forty-five dollars. Take it away: three hundred minus forty-five is two hundred and fifty-five dollars. Or three hundred times zero point eight five, which is also two hundred and fifty-five.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:35.923
Remember the method: find the base, decide up or down, find the amount, then add it or take it away. Now try the practice questions for this type.
